'''Kathleen Marie Hobbs''' (born December 28, 1969)<ref>{{cite tweet|author = Katie Hobbs|user = katiehobbs|number = 1608516319972069376|title = I got a new hat for my birthday yesterday: Soon To Be Governor|date = December 29, 2022|accessdate = January 28, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite tweet|author Team Katie Hobbs|user = TeamKatieHobbs|number = 1608145093378330625|title = Happy birthday Governor-Elect ⁦@katiehobbs!!! 53 going on 24th Governor of Arizona|date = December 28, 2022|accessdate = January 28, 2023}}</ref> is an American politician and social worker serving since 2023 as the 24th [[governor of Arizona]]. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], she served from 2019 to 2023 as [[Secretary of State of Arizona|Arizona's Secretary of State]] and from 2011 to 2019 as a member of the [[Arizona Legislature]].

Born and raised in [[Arizona]], Hobbs holds degrees in [[social work]] from [[Northern Arizona University]] and [[Arizona State University]]. She was elected to the [[Arizona House of Representatives]] in 2010 and to the [[Arizona Senate]] in 2012, where she served as Senate minority leader from 2015 to 2019. In [[2018 Arizona Secretary of State election|2018]], she was elected secretary of state of Arizona. In [[2022 Arizona gubernatorial election|2022]], Hobbs was elected governor, narrowly defeating Republican nominee [[Kari Lake]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-11-15 |title=Hobbs wins Arizona governor's race, flipping state for Dems |url=https://apnews.com/article/2022-midterm-elections-arizona-donald-trump-phoenix-doug-ducey-ceadb2bf55f1d5ec4760f423f1af0204 |access-date=2022-11-15 |website=AP NEWS |language=en}}</ref>

During her time in office, Hobbs has set the record for vetoes issued during a single legislative session as well as during a single term as governor.

==Early life, education, and career==
Hobbs was born on December 28, 1969, in [[Phoenix, Arizona]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://azgovernor.gov/governor/meet-governor-katie-hobbs |title=Meet Governor Katie Hobbs |date=December 11, 2014 |access-date=January 2, 2023}}</ref> She has a twin sister, Becky.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hobbs rallies supporters and states her priorities as Arizona governor |url=https://www.bizjournals.com/phoenix/news/2022/11/15/hobbs-arizona-governor-priorities.html |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=www.bizjournals.com}}</ref> A first-generation Arizonan, Hobbs grew up in [[Tempe, Arizona|Tempe]], an [[inner suburb]] of Phoenix. Her family was middle-class and sometimes relied on [[Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program|food stamps]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ulloa |first=Jazmine |date=2022-11-15 |title=Katie Hobbs, Who Defied Trump in Arizona, Tops Kari Lake for Governor |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/11/14/us/politics/katie-hobbs-arizona-governor.html |access-date=2022-11-18 |issn= 0362-4331}}</ref> She went to Catholic schools throughout her childhood and graduated from [[Seton Catholic Preparatory|Seton Catholic High School]] in 1988.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Reagan |first1=Kevin |title=Seton Catholic High celebrating 65 years of service |url=https://www.gilbertsunnews.com/news/seton-catholic-high-celebrating-years-of-service/article_3774994c-e6f6-11e9-9ced-737f09da9bae.html |publisher=Gilbert Sun News |date=October 6, 2019}}</ref>

Hobbs attended [[Northern Arizona University]], where she received a [[bachelor's degree]] in social work in 1992. She later attended [[Arizona State University]], where she received a [[master's degree]] in social work in 1995.<ref name="vote smart" /> She worked as a social worker for some time afterward.<ref>{{cite news |last1=MacDonald-Evoy |first1=Jerod |title=Mayes investigating Hobbs after GOP accuses gov of 'pay to play' |url=https://azmirror.com/briefs/mayes-investigating-hobbs-after-gop-accuses-gov-of-pay-to-play/ |access-date=28 February 2025 |work=Arizona Mirror |date=June 7, 2024}}</ref>

Hobbs was the chief compliance officer for Sojourner Center, one of the nation's largest domestic violence centers.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Crosby |first=Luann |title=LibGuides: Election: Arizona State Governor |url=https://npc.libguides.com/election/arizona-governor |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=npc.libguides.com |language=en |archive-date=November 18, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221118060049/https://npc.libguides.com/election/arizona-governor |url-status=dead }}</ref> She is an adjunct professor of social work at [[Paradise Valley Community College]] and Arizona State University.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Contact Katie Hobbs {{!}} Contact My Politician |url=https://www.contactmypolitician.com/about-politician/2207/contact-katie-hobbs |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=www.contactmypolitician.com}}</ref> She is affiliated with the [[National Association of Social Workers]] professional organization.<ref>{{cite web |title=Katie Hobbs - Arizona State Library, Archives and Public Records |url=https://apps.azlibrary.gov/officials/Legislators/Person/1551 |website=apps.azlibrary.gov |access-date=15 March 2026}}</ref>

== Early political career ==
Before seeking elected office, Hobbs participated in political leadership programs in multiple organizations, including Valley Leadership, Emerge Arizona, and the [[Center for Progressive Leadership]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Senate Member |url=https://www.azleg.gov/senate-member/?legislature=53&legislator=1797 |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=www.azleg.gov}}{{dead link|date=February 2025}}</ref> She was a delegate for [[Hillary Clinton]] at the [[2008 Democratic National Convention]].<ref name="Barchenger">{{Cite web |last=Barchenger |first=Stacey |title=Who is Katie Hobbs? 5 things to know about Arizona's Democratic governor |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2022/08/04/who-katie-hobbs-what-know-arizona-governor-nominee/10231959002/ |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=The Arizona Republic |language=en-US}}</ref>

Hobbs served on the Phoenix Women's Commission and the Phoenix Human Services Commission.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Katie Hobbs, Secretary of State from Arizona |url=http://voterly.com/politicians/IVTj2LEmxa/katie-hobbs |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=Voterly |language=en}}{{dead link|date=February 2025}}</ref>

Hobbs was the executive director of Emerge Arizona from 2013 to 2019.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Katie Hobbs {{!}} C-SPAN.org |url=https://www.c-span.org/person/?98128/KatieHobbs |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=www.c-span.org}}</ref> Hobbs credited her interest in politics to her involvement with Emerge Arizona.<ref>{{cite web |last=Richmond |first=Riva |date=March 7, 2018 |title=Running Women Q&A: Katie Hobbs is Itching to Fight Voter Suppression |url=https://thestoryexchange.org/running-women-qa-katie-hobbs-itching-fight-voter-suppression/ |website=thestoryexchange.org}}</ref>

=== Arizona House of Representatives ===
[[File:Katie Hobbs by Gage Skidmore.jpg|thumb|right|175px|Hobbs in 2015, speaking at a Phoenix event]]
Hobbs was elected to the [[Arizona House of Representatives]] in 2010. She represented the [[Arizona's 15th legislative district|15th legislative district]] alongside [[Lela Alston]].<ref name="New lawmaker">{{cite web |url= http://azcapitoltimes.com/news/2011/02/09/new-lawmaker-profile-rep-katie-hobbs-d-phoenix/ |first=Alyson|last= Zepeda |title= New lawmaker profile: Rep. Katie Hobbs, D-Phoenix |access-date= September 5, 2013 |work= [[Arizona Capitol Times]] |date= February 9, 2011}}</ref>

Hobbs served one term in the House of Representatives and ran for state senate in 2012.

=== Arizona Senate ===
Hobbs was elected to the [[Arizona Senate]] in 2012 and reelected in 2014 and 2016.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Katie Hobbs |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Katie_Hobbs |access-date=2023-12-29 |website=Ballotpedia |language=en}}</ref> She represented the [[Arizona's 24th legislative district|24th legislative district]]. She originally did not want to run for state senate but did so due to [[redistricting]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ehrler |first=Scout |title=Connecting with Secretary of State Katie Hobbs |url=https://www.jackcentral.org/news/connecting-with-secretary-of-state-katie-hobbs/article_e8f3098f-e1ac-5028-9ad2-7e56ec6834db.html |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=The Lumberjack |date=April 2, 2020 |language=en}}</ref> Hobbs became [[minority leader]] in 2015 and served two terms in that position.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.azleg.gov/senate-member/?legislature=53&session=119&legislator=1797|title=Senate Member - Katie Hobbs|access-date=December 1, 2021|website=azleg.gov}}</ref>

In 2015, during her first term as minority leader, Senate staffer Talonya Adams, a Black woman, complained to Hobbs about her concerns about racial and gender-based discrimination and was later fired in part by Hobbs.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://theshow.kjzz.org/content/1732462/black-staffer-arizona-senate-wins-275m-judgement-discrimination-case|title=A Black staffer in the Arizona Senate wins $2.75M judgement in discrimination case|date=November 11, 2021|website=kjzz.org|access-date=December 1, 2021|archive-date=December 1, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211201234301/https://theshow.kjzz.org/content/1732462/black-staffer-arizona-senate-wins-275m-judgement-discrimination-case|url-status=bot: unknown}}</ref> In November 2021, Adams won a [[discrimination]] lawsuit related to her firing and was awarded a judgment of $2.75 million.<ref>{{cite web|first=Jonathan J.|last=Cooper|url=https://apnews.com/article/business-arizona-discrimination-race-and-ethnicity-racial-injustice-47419004f504a0186a3298e0f0a99ff4|title=Fired Senate staffer awarded millions in discrimination suit|date=November 11, 2021|website=[[apnews.com]]}}</ref>

Hobbs did not run for another term in the State Senate, deciding to run for Secretary of State. She was succeeded by Alston.

== Secretary of State of Arizona ==

=== 2018 election ===
{{see also|2018 Arizona Secretary of State election}}
[[File:Katie Hobbs (32791539368).jpg|thumb|right|230px|Hobbs sworn in as Secretary of State in 2019]]

On March 8, 2017, Hobbs announced her candidacy for [[Secretary of State of Arizona|Arizona secretary of state]].<ref>{{cite web|first=Yvonne Wingett |last=Sanchez|url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2017/03/08/democratic-state-sen-katie-hobbs-seek-unseat-arizona-secretary-state-michele-reagan/98888628/|title=Democratic state Sen. Katie Hobbs will seek to unseat Arizona Secretary of State Michele Reagan|date=March 8, 2017|work=[[Arizona Republic]]}}</ref> In the [[2018 Arizona Secretary of State election|2018 election]], she faced Republican nominee Steve Gaynor. On November 6, 2018, the [[Associated Press]] prematurely called the race for Gaynor.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://azcapitoltimes.com/news/2018/11/06/arizona-katie-hobbs-steve-gaynor-secretary-of-states-race-is-close-as-election-results-pour-in/|title=AP: Steve Gaynor Arizona's next Secretary of State|last=Forman|first=Carmen|website=[[Arizona Capitol Times]]|language=en-US|date=November 6, 2018|access-date=November 30, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://tucson.com/news/local/arizona-election-results-roundup/article_93c0119a-e24c-11e8-9660-1ba3b3301933.html|title=Arizona Election Results Roundup|work=[[Arizona Daily Star]]|access-date=November 30, 2018|language=en}}</ref> With the race as close as it was, neither Hobbs nor Gaynor initially claimed victory.<ref>{{Cite tweet|first=Katie|last=Hobbs|user=katiehobbs|url=https://twitter.com/katiehobbs/status/1060050718244007936|title=I'm in this to win it and it's still too close to call. Arizona voters deserve to know their votes are still being counted.|number=1060050718244007936|work=Twitter|date=November 6, 2018|access-date=November 30, 2018|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2018/11/09/steve-gaynor-katie-hobbs-arizona-secretary-state-updated-race-results-election/1945034002/|title=Katie Hobbs, Steve Gaynor in dead heat to become Arizona's next secretary of state|work=[[Arizona Republic]]|date=November 9, 2018|access-date=November 30, 2018|language=en}}</ref> In subsequent days, Gaynor's lead narrowed as more ballots were counted. On November 16, Hobbs was officially declared the winner by a margin of 20,000 votes.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://results.arizona.vote/#/featured/4/0|title=Arizona Election Results|website=results.arizona.vote|access-date=November 30, 2018}}</ref><ref name="azcentral">{{Cite news|url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2018/11/16/katie-hobbs-apparent-winner-arizona-secretary-states-race-steve-gaynor/2029677002/|title=Gov. Doug Ducey congratulates Katie Hobbs on winning Arizona secretary of state's race|work=[[Arizona Republic]]|date=November 16, 2018|access-date=November 16, 2018|language=en}}</ref> She was the first Democrat to hold the post since [[Richard Mahoney]] left office in 1995.<ref name="azcentral"/>

=== Tenure as secretary of state ===
Hobbs was sworn in as secretary of state on January 7, 2019. Because Arizona has no lieutenant governor, Hobbs stood first in the line of succession to Governor [[Doug Ducey]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.azleg.gov/viewDocument/?docName=http://www.azleg.gov/const/5/6.htm|title=Constitution of Arizona: Article V, Section 6|work=[[Arizona Legislature]]|access-date=August 22, 2019}}</ref>

==== 2020 Arizona election audit ====
{{main|2021 Maricopa County presidential ballot audit}}

In 2021, the Arizona Senate Republicans provided $150,000 to fund an audit aimed at contesting the [[U.S. presidential election, 2020|2020 presidential election]] results in [[Maricopa County, Arizona|Maricopa County]]. In a six-page letter, Hobbs wrote that the audit's chain of custody was lacking, calling it "a significant departure from standard best practices".<ref name=NYT>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/06/us/arizona-vote-count-republicans.html|title=Arizona Review of 2020 Vote Is Riddled With Flaws, Says Secretary of State|newspaper=[[The New York Times]]|first=Michael|last=Wines|date=May 6, 2021|accessdate=May 16, 2021}}</ref> She added that the audit procedures appeared "better suited for chasing conspiracy theories than as a part of a professional audit".<ref name=NYT/> In response, Hobbs received death threats, and the Arizona Department of Public Safety assigned personnel to guard her and her staff.<ref>{{cite web|first1=Anna-Maja|last1=Rappard|first2=Paul|last2=LeBlanc|date=May 7, 2021|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/05/07/politics/katie-hobbs-arizona-secretary-of-state-death-threats/index.html|title=Arizona secretary of state assigned protection following death threats amid election audit|website=[[CNN]]|accessdate=May 16, 2021}}</ref>
[[File:Katie Hobbs by Gage Skidmore 3.jpg|175px|thumb|Hobbs in 2022 at a Phoenix event]]
Hobbs's complaints were echoed in a letter to State Senate President [[Karen Fann]] from the [[Maricopa County Board of Supervisors]], which had a 4-1 Republican majority. Fann, referring to one of [[Donald Trump]]'s false [[Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election|claims of election fraud]],<ref name="News-1">{{Cite news |last=Broadwater |first=Luke |date=2026-07-17 |title=Multiple Investigations Refuted Trump's Claims That Fraud Altered the Outcome in 2020 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/16/us/politics/trump-investigations-fraud-claims-2020-election.html |access-date=2026-10-14 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> contended that the county had deleted an entire database. The board of supervisors responded in a letter, calling the accusations "false, defamatory, and beneath the dignity of the Senate". It accused the Arizona Senate of "conspiracy theories that fuel the fundraising schemes of those pulling your strings".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/may/16/arizona-republican-trump-deleted-database-statement-unhinged|title=Arizona Republican calls Trump 'deleted database' statement 'unhinged'|newspaper=[[The Guardian]]|first=Sam|last=Levine|date=May 16, 2021|accessdate=May 18, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/may/14/arizona-election-audit-recount-ballots-maricopa-county|title=Cyber Ninjas, UV lights and far-right funding: inside the strange Arizona 2020 election 'audit'|newspaper=[[The Guardian]]|first=Sam|last=Levine|date=May 14, 2021|accessdate=May 18, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2021/05/18/fact-check-false-claim-trump-maricopa-county-election-data/5119684001/|title=Fact check-False claim from Trump about Maricopa County election database|newspaper=[[USA Today]]|first=Daniel|last=Funke|date=May 18, 2021|accessdate=May 19, 2021}}</ref> Fann responded that the audit would continue when the [[Arizona Veterans Memorial Coliseum]] site was next available.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/maricopa-county-audit-senate/2021/05/18/b6fab370-b816-11eb-a5fe-bb49dc89a248_story.html|title=Arizona Senate president says 2020 recount would continue, despite strident objections from Maricopa County officials|newspaper=[[Washington Post]]|first=Rosalind S.|last=Helderman|date=May 18, 2021|accessdate=May 19, 2020}}</ref>

After consulting with the [[United States Department of Homeland Security|Department of Homeland Security]] and the [[Election Assistance Commission]], Hobbs said she was told that it was impossible to know whether the voting machines the county turned over in response to the Senate subpoena had been compromised and that [[Dominion Voting Systems]] should recertify them for future use.<ref name=compromised/> While the Arizona Senate's contractor was in possession of the machines that had been subpoenaed, the county spent over $20,000 to lease other machines to conduct two local elections, and the costs of recertifying the surrendered machines after their return would be in the six-figure range; however, the Senate signed an agreement with the county that said the county is not liable for any damages to the equipment while in the Senate's custody, so it is unclear whether the county would be liable for the costs.<ref name=compromised>{{cite news|url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2021/05/20/katie-hobbs-may-not-let-maricopa-co-reuse-voting-machines-after-audit/5185817001/|title=Arizona secretary of state may not let Maricopa County reuse voting machines after audit|newspaper=[[Arizona Republic]]|first=Jen|last=Fifield|date=May 20, 2021|accessdate=May 22, 2021}}</ref>

The audit was conducted by Florida-based company Cyber Ninjas, which had no previous experience in election audits and had not been certified by the federal government to conduct election audits.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2021-05-12 |title=Arizona recount: Why Republicans are still tallying votes |language=en-GB |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-57089359 |access-date=2022-11-18}}</ref> Cyber Ninjas' owner, Doug Logan, supported Trump and promoted Trump's false claims of election fraud.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Teh |first=Cheryl |title=Doug Logan, the man behind the Cyber Ninjas Arizona audit, starred in an election conspiracy theory film alleging the CIA was behind election misinformation |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/doug-logan-cyber-ninjas-arizona-audit-election-conspiracy-theory-film-2021-6 |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=Business Insider |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name="News-1" /> The auditors released a report in September 2021, finding no proof of fraud and that their ballot recount increased Biden's margin of victory by 360 votes.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rappard |first=Eric Bradner, Anna-Maja |date=2021-09-24 |title=Final report from partisan Arizona review confirms Biden defeated Trump in Maricopa County last November {{!}} CNN Politics |url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/09/24/politics/arizona-election-review-results/index.html |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Healy |first1=Jack |last2=Wines |first2=Michael |last3=Corasaniti |first3=Nick |date=2021-09-24 |title=Republican Review of Arizona Vote Fails to Show Stolen Election |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/24/us/arizona-election-review-trump-biden.html |access-date=2022-11-18 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

==Governor of Arizona==
=== 2022 election===
{{main|2022 Arizona gubernatorial election}}

[[File:2022 Arizona gubernatorial election results map by county.svg|210px|thumb|County results of Hobbs's gubernatorial win in 2022]]
On June 2, 2021, Hobbs announced her candidacy for [[List of governors of Arizona|governor of Arizona]] in the [[2022 Arizona gubernatorial election|2022 election]] to succeed term-limited Republican incumbent [[Doug Ducey]].<ref name="mondaymusings">{{cite news|last1=Herstam|first1=Chris|date=January 6, 2020|title=Katie Hobbs for governor – already?|website=[[The Times-Gazette]]|url=https://www.azmirror.com/2020/01/06/monday-musings-all-eyes-on-how-sinema-handles-impeachment/|access-date=October 25, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Rosenblatt|first=Dillon|date=2021-05-21|title=Races for 2022 statewide offices taking shape|url=https://azcapitoltimes.com/news/2021/05/21/races-for-2022-statewide-offices-taking-shape/|access-date=2021-05-25|website=Arizona Capitol Times|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Oxford|first=Andrew|title=In spotlight of audit, Secretary of State Katie Hobbs announces run for Arizona governor|url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2021/06/02/arizona-secretary-state-katie-hobbs-announces-bid-governor/7492278002/|access-date=2021-06-02|website=The Arizona Republic|language=en-US}}</ref>

Hobbs ran against former [[U.S. Customs and Border Protection|Customs and Border Protection]] chief of staff [[Marco A. Lopez Jr.|Marco Lopez]] and former state representative [[Aaron Lieberman]] in the Democratic primary. Despite declining to debate her opponents, she won the Democratic nomination with 72.3% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.kawc.org/news/2022-08-02/lopez-concedes-democratic-primary-for-governor | title=Lopez Concedes Democratic Primary for Governor, Hobbs Says Time to Look Ahead | date=August 3, 2022 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-08-02 |title=Ahead of the primary, Hobbs looking forward to the general election |url=https://www.abc15.com/news/political/elections/ahead-of-the-primary-hobbs-looking-forward-to-the-general-election |access-date=2022-10-24 |website=ABC15 Arizona in Phoenix (KNXV) |language=en}}</ref>

Hobbs faced the Republican nominee, former [[KSAZ-TV]] news anchor [[Kari Lake]], in the general election. Hobbs limited access to reporters, sometimes going out of her way to avoid them, and held small-scale campaign events.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Reston |first=Maeve |date=2022-11-15 |title=Katie Hobbs will win Arizona governor's race, CNN projects, defeating Trump favorite Kari Lake {{!}} CNN Politics |url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/11/14/politics/katie-hobbs-arizona-governors-race/index.html |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Healy |first=Jack |date=2022-10-21 |title=In Arizona Governor's Race, a Question Looms: 'Where's Katie?' |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/10/21/us/az-governor-katie-hobbs-kari-lake.html |access-date=2022-11-18 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> She declined to debate Lake, saying she wanted to deny Lake the opportunity to spread [[Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election|election denialism]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mizelle |first=Shawna |date=2022-11-02 |title=Democrat Hobbs defends decision not to debate GOP's Lake in tight Arizona gubernatorial race {{!}} CNN Politics |url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/11/02/politics/katie-hobbs-debate-arizona-kari-lake-midterms-cnntv/index.html |access-date=2022-11-11 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Christie |first=Bob |date=2022-09-11 |title=Democrat Katie Hobbs says no to Arizona governor debate |url=https://apnews.com/article/2022-midterm-elections-arizona-campaigns-government-and-politics-540ee7c3603d522c4e12c4e7083443b5 |access-date=2022-09-12 |work=[[Associated Press]] |language=en}}</ref> Hobbs narrowly defeated Lake with 50.3% of the vote.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Democrat Katie Hobbs defeats MAGA favorite Kari Lake in high-stakes race for governor in Arizona |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2022-election/democrat-katie-hobbs-defeats-maga-favorite-kari-lake-high-stakes-race-rcna55172 |access-date=2022-11-15 |website=NBC News |date=November 15, 2022 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=2022-11-08 |title=Arizona Governor Election Results |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/11/08/us/elections/results-arizona-governor.html |access-date=2022-11-18 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> After the election, Lake refused to concede and assembled a legal team to contest the election results.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-11-17 |title=Election denier Kari Lake refuses to concede Arizona governor race she lost |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/17/kari-lake-refuse-concede-arizona-governor-election-republican |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=the Guardian |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Berzon |first1=Alexandra |last2=Rutenberg |first2=Jim |date=2022-11-17 |title=Kari Lake says she is 'exploring every avenue' to fight her loss, despite no sign of election-tilting problems. |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/11/17/us/elections/kari-lake-arizona-election.html |access-date=2022-11-18 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Schonfeld |first=Zach |date=2022-11-17 |title=Kari Lake declines to concede, says she's assembling legal team |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/3739934-kari-lake-declines-to-concede-says-shes-assembling-legal-team/ |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=The Hill |language=en-US}}</ref> In March 2023, the [[Arizona Supreme Court]] declined to hear Lake's lawsuit concerning the election,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Vigdor |first1=Neil |title=Arizona Supreme Court Turns Down Kari Lake's Appeal in Her Election Lawsuit |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/03/23/us/politics/kari-lake-arizona-supreme-court.html |access-date=March 24, 2023 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20230323192116/https://www.nytimes.com/2023/03/23/us/politics/kari-lake-arizona-supreme-court.html |archive-date=March 23, 2023 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=March 23, 2023}}</ref> and in May reaffirmed its decision after a trial.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Barchenger |first1=Stacey |title=Judge rules against Kari Lake, affirms Hobbs as AZ governor in election signature verification trial |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2023/05/22/judge-rules-against-kari-lake-in-arizona-governor-election-trial/70244231007/ |archive-url=https://archive.today/20230523034637/https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2023/05/22/judge-rules-against-kari-lake-in-arizona-governor-election-trial/70244231007/ |archive-date=May 23, 2023 |work=[[The Arizona Republic]] |access-date=May 23, 2023 |date=May 22, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Lah |first1=Kyung |title=Arizona judge rejects Kari Lake's final 2022 election lawsuit |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2023/05/23/politics/arizona-kari-lake-election-lawsuit/index.html |access-date=May 23, 2023 |work=[[CNN]] |date=May 23, 2023}}</ref>

=== Tenure ===
Hobbs was sworn in on January 2, 2023, in a private ceremony, followed by a public ceremony on January 5.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://wtop.com/government/2023/01/democrat-katie-hobbs-to-take-office-as-arizona-governor/ | title=Democrat Katie Hobbs takes office as Arizona governor | work=WTOP News | date=January 3, 2023 }}</ref> Upon taking office, she became Arizona's fifth female governor, a record for [[U.S. state]]s.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Fink |first=Erik |title=Katie Hobbs gives victory speech, will become fifth female governor in Arizona |url=https://www.kvoa.com/townnews/politics/katie-hobbs-gives-victory-speech-will-become-fifth-female-governor-in-arizona/article_a69b4530-6567-11ed-b15c-ef3a377ed476.html |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=KVOA |date=November 15, 2022 |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite web |last=Barchenger |first=Stacey |title=Katie Hobbs elected Arizona's 5th female governor, defeating election denier Kari Lake |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2022/11/14/katie-hobbs-elected-arizona-governor-defeating-kari-lake/10700050002/ |access-date=2022-11-15 |website=The Arizona Republic |language=en-US}}</ref> In December 2022, she selected Allie Bones, the Arizona assistant secretary of state, as her chief of staff.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-11-22 |title=Arizona Gov.-elect Katie Hobbs appoints Allie Bones as chief of staff |url=https://ktar.com/story/5358539/arizona-gov-elect-katie-hobbs-names-assistant-sec-of-state-allie-bones-as-chief-of-staff/ |access-date=2022-12-24 |website=KTAR.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Levitt |first=Shannon |date=December 21, 2022 |title=A Jewish woman will soon hold powerful and prized job in Arizona politics |url=https://www.jewishaz.com/community/a-jewish-woman-will-soon-hold-powerful-and-prized-job-in-arizona-politics/article_13fcf9c2-816d-11ed-9314-03adfab9a568.html |access-date=2022-12-24 |website=Jewish News |language=en}}</ref> Bones resigned on May 25, 2023, and was replaced by [[Chad Campbell (politician)|Chad Campbell]], the former minority leader of the Arizona House of Representatives, on May 31.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Knox |first=Brady |date=May 25, 2023 |title=Arizona Gov. Katie Hobbs chief of staff Allie Bones resigns |url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/politics/arizona-gov-katie-hobbs-chief-of-staff-allie-bones-resigns/ar-AA1bHQeZ |access-date=2023-05-26 |website=[[MSN]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sievers |first=Caitlin |date=May 25, 2023 |title=Allie Bones, chief of staff to Katie Hobbs, resigns after less than 5 months on the job |url=https://www.azmirror.com/blog/allie-bones-chief-of-staff-to-katie-hobbs-resigns-after-less-than-5-months-on-the-job/ |access-date=2023-05-26 |website=AZMIRROR |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Duba |first=Jeremy |date=May 31, 2023 |title=Hobbs picks former House Dem leader Campbell as new chief of staff |url=https://www.axios.com/local/phoenix/2023/05/31/arizona-katie-hobbs-chad-campbell-new-chief-of-staff |access-date=2023-05-31 |website=[[Axios (website)|Axios]] |language=en}}</ref>
[[File:Katie Hobbs (52668036258).jpg|thumb|250px|Hobbs speaking at an event in [[Surprise, Arizona]], February 2023]]

Since taking office, Hobbs has issued numerous executive orders, including those prohibiting state agencies and all new state contracts or subcontracts from discrimination based on traits of sexual orientation or [[gender identity]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Arizona Gov. Issues Executive Order Protecting LGBTQ+ Workers |url=https://www.advocate.com/politics/2023/1/06/arizona-gov-issues-executive-order-protecting-lgbtq-workers |access-date=2023-04-19 |website=www.advocate.com |language=en}}</ref> Hobbs has established several commissions, including an independent prison oversight commission;<ref>{{Cite web |last=Crane |first=Steve |date=2023-01-26 |title=Arizona Gov. Katie Hobbs creates independent prison oversight commission |url=https://cronkitenews.azpbs.org/2023/01/25/arizona-gov-katie-hobbs-creates-independent-prison-oversight-commission/ |access-date=2023-04-19 |website=Cronkite News - Arizona PBS |language=en-US}}</ref> a commission on homelessness and housing that was abolished in 2020; a bipartisan elections task force;<ref>{{Cite web |last=KTAR.com |first=ALEX WEINER |date=2023-01-07 |title=Arizona Gov. Hobbs signs executive orders on homelessness, elections |url=https://ktar.com/?p=5405302 |access-date=2023-04-19 |website=KTAR.com |language=en}}</ref> and a task force on [[Missing and Murdered Indigenous Women]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hobbs signs executive order establishing MMIP Task Force |url=https://www.nhonews.com/news/2023/mar/21/hobbs-signs-executive-order-establishing-mmip-task/ |access-date=2023-04-19 |website=Navajo-Hopi Observer News |date=March 21, 2023 |language=en}}</ref>

In May 2023, the [[Russia]]n government permanently banned Hobbs from entering Russia.<ref>{{cite news |last1=MacDonald-Evoy |first1=Jerod |title=Katie Hobbs has been banned from Russia |url=https://www.azmirror.com/blog/katie-hobbs-has-been-banned-from-russia/ |access-date=May 27, 2023 |publisher=AZMIRROR |date=May 23, 2023}}</ref><ref name="RUSSIA">{{cite news |last1=Lauterbach |first1=Cole |title=Hobbs, GOP congressmen, ASU president among 500 banned from Russia |url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/politics/hobbs-gop-congressmen-asu-president-among-500-banned-from-russia/ar-AA1bLApo |access-date=May 27, 2023 |publisher=[[Washington Examiner]] |date=May 16, 2023}}</ref> The banning came after the Biden administration imposed further sanctions on Russia; others banned from entering Russia include U.S. Representative [[Eli Crane]] and [[Arizona State University]] president [[Michael M. Crow]].<ref name="RUSSIA"/>

In June 2024, [[Arizona Attorney General]] [[Kris Mayes]], a Democrat, opened an investigation into Hobbs after a group home business whose CEO was on the governor's inaugural committee donated $400,000 to her campaign through a [[dark money]] group.<ref name=":02">{{Cite web |last=MacDonald-Evoy |first=Jerod |date=June 7, 2024 |title=Mayes investigating Hobbs after GOP accuses gov of 'pay to play' |url=https://azmirror.com/briefs/mayes-investigating-hobbs-after-gop-accuses-gov-of-pay-to-play/ |access-date=2025-07-02 |website=Arizona Mirror |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Barchenger |first=Stacey |date=June 7, 2024 |title=This Arizona group home donated $400,000 to Gov. Katie Hobbs, Democrats and got more state money |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2024/06/05/arizona-group-home-gives-big-to-governor-katie-hobbs-gets-more-state-money/73957477007/ |website=AZcentral.com |publisher=Arizona Republic}}</ref> The firm received nearly a 60% rate increase for providing [[foster care]] services, making it Arizona's highest-paid provider per day per child.<ref name=":02" /> A spokesman for Hobbs blamed partisan politics for the investigation.<ref name=":02" /><ref name=":1" /> In March 2026, the Arizona state senate passed legislation intended to provide more transparency about state contractors' contributions to political campaigns in the wake of the scandal.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Duda |first1=Jeremy |title=Arizona Senate passes "pay-to-play" reform bill |url=https://www.axios.com/local/phoenix/2026/03/06/pay-to-play-ethics-reform-bill-hobbs-shope |access-date=6 March 2026 |work=Axios |date=6 March 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Schutsky |first1=Wayne |title=Arizona Senate passes Republican 'pay-to-play' reform bill, not Hobbs' plan |url=https://www.kjzz.org/politics/2026-03-03/arizona-senate-passes-republican-pay-to-play-reform-bill-not-hobbs-plan |access-date=6 March 2026 |work=KJZZ |date=3 March 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

====Abortion====
{{Main|Abortion in Arizona}}

On May 2, 2024, Hobbs signed a bill to repeal a near-total [[abortion]] ban from 1864 that the Arizona Supreme Court reinstated on April 9, 2024.<ref>{{Cite web |last=John |first=Arit |date=2024-05-02 |title=Arizona Democratic governor signs bill repealing 1864 abortion ban {{!}} CNN Politics |url=https://www.cnn.com/2024/05/02/politics/arizona-abortion-repeal-governor/index.html |access-date=2024-05-05 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref> The ban was not enforced by [[Arizona Attorney General]] [[Kris Mayes]], and the repeal took effect on September 14, 2024.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Govindarao |first1=Sejal |date=September 14, 2024 |title=Arizona's 1864 abortion ban is officially off the books |url=https://apnews.com/article/arizona-abortion-ban-repeal-ac4a1eb97efcd3c506aeaac8f8152127 |website=The Associated Press}}</ref> As a result of [[2024 Arizona Proposition 139]], the Arizona constitution codifies legal abortion up to the point of [[fetal viability]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-11-06 |title=Arizona voters enshrine abortion rights in state constitution |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2024-election/arizona-ballot-measure-proposition-139-amendment-passes-rcna173895 |access-date=2024-11-06 |website=NBC News |language=en}}</ref>

====Capital punishment====
An opponent of [[Capital punishment in Arizona|capital punishment]], Hobbs voted for an unsuccessful bill in the state legislature to repeal Arizona's death penalty, and tweeted, "both heinous crimes and the death penalty demean us as a society". As governor, Hobbs has said that her opinion of the death penalty is "irrelevant", and in December 2024, she said she was "not going to talk about" her views on the death penalty, saying that the death penalty is the law in Arizona and that she "is going to follow the law".<ref>{{Cite web |date= April 1, 2023 |title= Senate Member, Katie Hobbs, Democratic, District 24 |url= https://www.azleg.gov/senate-member/?legislature=53&legislator=1797 |access-date= 2025-10-19 |website= Arizona State Legislature |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title= Gov. Hobbs, Attorney General Mayes pause death penalty in Arizona pending review process |url= https://eu.azcentral.com/story/news/local/arizona/2023/01/20/gov-hobbs-ag-mayes-pause-death-penalty-in-arizona-pending-review/69825942007/ |publisher= azcentral |date= October 19, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title= Katie Hobbs on X: "It is possible to hold the simultaneous conviction that both heinous crimes and the death penalty demean us as a society." |url= https://x.com/katiehobbs/status/492158847021514753 |access-date= 2026-08-31 |website= x.com}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title= Gov. Hobbs stands by decision to fire expert who reviewed state execution practices |url= https://azcapitoltimes.com/news/2024/12/09/gov-hobbs-stands-by-decision-to-fire-expert-who-reviewed-state-execution-practices/ |publisher= Arizona Capitol Times |date= October 19, 2025}}</ref> When Hobbs took office, she [[Moratorium (law)|halted executions]] and was ordered to appear alongside Ryan Thornell, Arizona's prison director, in Maricopa County Superior Court, over her refusal to carry out an execution warrant issued by the Arizona Supreme Court.<ref>{{Cite web |date= April 1, 2023 |title= Arizona governor must appear in court on pause of executions |url= https://apnews.com/article/execution-death-row-pause-arizona-44b733f78d142d72087fce30a94f2f6e |access-date= 2023-04-02 |website= AP News |language= en}}</ref> On January 20, 2023, she ordered a review of the state's death penalty protocols, and hired retired magistrate Judge David Duncan to carry it out.<ref>{{cite news |last1= Billeaud |first1= Jaques |title= Arizona executions on hold amid review ordered by governor |url= https://apnews.com/article/arizona-state-government-crime-legal-proceedings-katie-hobbs-ef16f2efb25e68bd7df2860b75691645 |access-date= March 16, 2023 |publisher= Associated Press |date= January 20, 2023}}</ref> Arizona conservatives criticized Hobbs for not cooperating with the court-ordered execution of [[Aaron Gunches]] in 2023.<ref>{{cite news |last1= Lewis |first1= Kaitlin |title= Arizona Governor Delays Execution of Inmate Who Requested the Death Penalty |url= https://www.newsweek.com/arizona-governor-delays-execution-inmate-who-requested-death-penalty-1788375 |access-date= May 26, 2023 |publisher= [[Newsweek]] |date= March 16, 2023}}</ref> After Thornell completed an internal review, Hobbs reversed her executive order that effectively halted executions by ending the review she commissioned Duncan to complete and firing Duncan; shortly thereafter, Attorney General [[Kris Mayes]] requested an execution date for Gunches, who was executed on March 19, 2025. This was the first execution conducted in a state with a Democratic governor in office since [[Virginia]] executed [[William Morva]] under Governor [[Terry McAuliffe]] in 2017.<ref>{{cite news |last1= Billeaud |first1= Jaques |title= Arizona executes a man who murdered his girlfriend's ex-husband |url= https://apnews.com/article/arizona-execution-aaron-gunches-ted-price-e415c25a244be5a82ce0ee586990244e |access-date= March 20, 2025 |publisher= [[Associated Press]] |date= March 19, 2025}}</ref> On October 17, 2025, [[Richard Djerf]] became the second person executed in Arizona during Hobbs's governorship, and the first to have exhausted his appellate rights (Gunches waived most of his appeals).<ref>{{cite news| url= https://www.cbsnews.com/news/arizona-execution-richard-djerf-phoenix-family-1993/| title= Arizona executes man convicted of killing 4 family members in 1993 Phoenix murders| work= [[CBS News]]| agency= [[Associated Press]]| date= October 17, 2025| access-date= October 17, 2025}}</ref> On May 20, 2026, [[Murder of Charles Perez|Leroy McGill]] was executed.<ref name= "ktarfm">{{cite news |last1= O'Sullivan |first1= Serena |title= Arizona man convicted of killing victim by setting him on fire scheduled for execution |url= http://ktar.com/arizona-news/execution-death-penalty-mcgill-arson/5840510/ |access-date= 1 May 2026 |work= [[KTAR-FM]] |date= 26 March 2026}}</ref>

====Immigration====
In her 2023 [[State of the State address]], Hobbs proposed expanding the Arizona Promise Scholarship Program to make it available for undocumented immigrant students to attend state universities and colleges.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Zetino |first=Griselda |title=Creating scholarship program for 'Dreamers' is a top priority for Arizona Gov. Hobbs |url=https://ktar.com/story/5451204/arizona-gov-hobbs-said-a-college-scholarship-for-dreamers-is-top-priority/ |work=KtarNews.com |date=February 16, 2023 |language=en}}</ref>

In May 2023, ahead of the repeal of [[Title 42 expulsion|Title 42]], Hobbs announced that the state would establish five new bus routes to transport migrants from small border communities to [[Tucson, Arizona|Tucson]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Gov. Katie Hobbs announces expanded migrant bus routes ahead of Title 42's end |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/border-issues/2023/05/09/hobbs-says-migrant-bus-routes-7-million-part-of-arizona-response-to-title-42-end/70199909007/ |access-date=2023-05-26 |website=Az Central |language=en}}</ref> 

On December 17, 2023, Hobbs issued an executive order ordering the [[Arizona National Guard]] to the border with Mexico to help federal officials manage an influx of migrants.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-12-16 |title=Arizona's governor is sending the state's National Guard to the border to help with a migrant influx |url=https://apnews.com/article/arizona-border-national-guard-7f222b37ac7504bea0f0ef6a2f387ef0 |access-date=2024-01-04 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref>

In 2025, Hobbs vetoed Senate Bill 1164, which would have prevented state agencies and local governments from adopting policies banning cooperation with federal immigration authorities. It would have also required sheriffs and the state prison system to comply with detainer requests from the federal government.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dowd |first=Bridget |date=2025-04-19 |title=Gov. Hobbs vetoes Republican-backed Arizona ICE Act |url=https://www.kjzz.org/politics/2025-04-19/gov-hobbs-vetoes-republican-backed-arizona-ice-act |access-date=2026-04-24 |website=KJZZ |language=en}}</ref> Hobbs has argued that immigration enforcement is primarily a federal responsibility, opposing state-level measures that would expand Arizona's role in policing immigration.

==== Housing ====
In March 2024, Hobbs vetoed bipartisan legislation to increase Arizona's housing supply.<ref name=":3">{{Cite web |date=2024-03-18 |title=Arizona Gov. Katie Hobbs vetoes bipartisan housing bill, citing 'unclear outcomes' |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2024/03/18/arizona-gov-katie-hobbs-vetoes-bipartisan-housing-bill/73017918007/ |website=The Arizona Republic |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Times |first=Jakob Thorington Arizona Capitol |date=2024-03-18 |title=Hobbs vetoes bipartisan housing legislation {{!}} Arizona Capitol Times |url=https://azcapitoltimes.com/news/2024/03/18/hobbs-vetoes-bipartisan-housing-legislation/ |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=MacDonald-Evoy |first=Jerod |date=2024-03-18 |title=Hobbs vetoes bill designed to jumpstart 'starter home' construction in AZ, citing 'unintended consequences' |url=https://azmirror.com/briefs/hobbs-vetoes-bill-designed-to-jumpstart-starter-home-construction-in-az-citing-unintended-consequences/ |website=Arizona Mirror |language=en-US}}</ref> The bill would have reduced red tape around housing construction by preventing Arizona municipalities from requiring homeowners' associations, minimum home sizes, and certain building setbacks.<ref name=":3" /> She said the bill was "a step too far" and had "unclear outcomes".<ref name=":3" /> The legislation's sponsors criticized Hobbs for not offering any guidance on the legislation when it was being negotiated, as they could have adjusted the bill's language to make it more in line with her preferences.<ref name=":3" />

====Medical debt forgiveness ====
In March 2024, Hobbs announced that Arizona would use $30 million of federal COVID-19 relief funds to forgive Arizonans' medical debts. Using the funds from the 2021 [[American Rescue Plan Act]], Arizona will task the RIP Medical Debt organization to buy medical debt held by hospitals and collection agencies. An RIP Medical Debt board member said that up to $2 billion of debt could be forgiven.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Barchenger |first1=Stacey |title=Gov. Katie Hobbs to erase medical debt for 1M Arizonans with COVID relief funding |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2024/03/04/arizona-governor-to-use-american-rescue-plan-act-money-to-erase-medical-debt-for-1m/72840751007/ |access-date=March 5, 2024 |work=[[Arizona Republic]] |date=March 4, 2024}}</ref>

==== Veto record ====
On April 18, 2023, her 100th day in office, Hobbs set a new record for the most vetoes issued by an Arizona governor in a single legislative session, with 63 vetoes of bills passed by the Republican-majority legislature. The previous record was set by former governor [[Janet Napolitano]], who vetoed 58 total bills in the 2005 session.<ref>{{Cite web |last=KTAR.COM |date=2023-04-18 |title=Katie Hobbs sets record for most vetoes in single legislative session |url=https://ktar.com/story/5485439/arizona-gov-katie-hobbs-sets-record-for-most-vetoes-in-single-legislative-session/ |access-date=2023-04-19 |website=KTAR.com |language=en}}</ref>

Three of the bills vetoed on April 18 passed with a bipartisan supermajority in both the Arizona House and the Arizona Senate.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Sievers |first1=Caitlin |title=Katie Hobbs just set Arizona's veto record — rejecting bills on elections, vaccines and firearms |url=https://www.azmirror.com/2023/04/18/katie-hobbs-just-set-arizonas-veto-record-rejecting-bills-on-elections-vaccines-and-firearms/ |website=Arizona Mirror |access-date=28 April 2023 |date=18 April 2023}}</ref> One of them, concerned with [[Putting-out system#Cottage industry|cottage foods]] and colloquially known as the "tamale bill", became a topic of national conversation after multiple Democratic legislators voiced their opposition to the veto. The bill would have allowed home cooks to sell perishable foods.<ref>{{cite web |title=The Hot Issue Bedeviling Arizona's New Governor: Tamales |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/04/25/us/arizona-tamales-katie-hobbs-veto.html |website=The New York Times |access-date=28 April 2023 |date=25 April 2023}}</ref> Although the bill passed with a bipartisan supermajority, a vote to override the veto in the Arizona House of Representatives failed, with only five Democrats voting to override.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Duda |first1=Jeremy |title=Override of "tamale bill" veto fails as Arizona Democrats back Gov. Katie Hobbs |url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/politics/override-of-tamale-bill-veto-fails-as-arizona-democrats-back-gov-katie-hobbs/ar-AA1albjG |website=Axios Phoenix |access-date=28 April 2023 |date=25 April 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Phillips |first1=Nick |last2=Thorington |first2=Jakob |last3=Camryn |first3=Sanchez |title=Override falls short after partisan fireworks |url=https://azcapitoltimes.com/news/2023/04/27/override-falls-short-after-partisan-fireworks/ |website=Arizona Capitol Times |access-date=28 April 2023 |date=27 April 2023}}</ref> The other two bills Hobbs vetoed that passed with supermajorities, SB1091 and SB1101, have not been brought forward to an override vote. On May 19, Hobbs vetoed 14 bills passed by the state house and senate, surpassing 100 vetoes in only five months; among them was HB2377, which would have restricted officials from being registered lobbyists while holding public office.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hobbs vetoes bill banning elected officials from lobbying |url=https://www.thecentersquare.com/arizona/article_2681e854-fa63-11ed-9635-ef89aa90753d.html |date=2023-05-24 |access-date=2023-05-26 |last=Moran |first=Carly |work=[[The Center Square]] |language=en}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Hobbs is married to Patrick Goodman, whom she met at church in 1992 and married in 1996.<ref name="Barchenger"/> Goodman is a child therapist at [[Phoenix Children's Hospital]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Patrick T. Goodman, LPC {{!}} Phoenix Children's Hospital |url=https://www.phoenixchildrens.org/find-a-doctor/patrick-t-goodman-lpc |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=www.phoenixchildrens.org |language=en}}</ref> They have two children and live in Phoenix.<ref name="Senate bio">{{cite web|title=Katie Hobbs|url=http://www.azleg.gov/MembersPage.asp?Member_ID=98&Legislature=51&Session_ID=110|agency=Arizona State Senate|access-date=September 5, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130225040005/http://www.azleg.gov/MembersPage.asp?Member_ID=98&Legislature=51&Session_ID=110|archive-date=February 25, 2013|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=The American Association for Marriage and Family Therapy – Secy. of State Katie Hobbs (D)|url=https://www.congressweb.com/aamft#/legislators/info/mbr_id/9303|access-date=December 15, 2020|website=CongressWeb.com|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221013140434/https://www.congressweb.com/aamft/legislators/info/mbr_id/9303|archive-date=October 13, 2022|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="vote smart">{{Cite web|title=Katie Hobbs' Biography|url=https://justfacts.votesmart.org/candidate/biography/123724/katie-hobbs|access-date=December 15, 2020|website=Vote Smart|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220607022029/https://justfacts.votesmart.org/candidate/biography/123724/katie-hobbs|archive-date=June 7, 2022|url-status=live}}</ref>

Hobbs is Catholic.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-01-11 |title=Arizona Gov. Katie Hobbs on her faith, her career and a bruising campaign |url=https://kjzz.org/content/1835828/arizona-gov-katie-hobbs-her-faith-her-career-and-bruising-campaign |access-date=2024-05-04 |website=KJZZ |language=en}}</ref> She is a [[Triathlon|triathlete]] and has been an avid [[Cycling|cyclist]] since high school.<ref name="Barchenger"/><ref>{{Cite web |last=Muench |first=Sarah |date=2019-03-18 |title=Arizona's Secretary of State rides bikes, will do Nogales Bicycle Classic |url=https://www.clippedin.bike/arizona-secretary-of-state-katie-hobbs-rides-bikes-cycling-cyclist-triathlete-nogales-bicycle-classic/ |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=Clipped In |language=en-US}}</ref>

